00:17:35,510 --> 00:17:41,095
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why you cannot, as we've said before, you cannot abandon things temporarily.

173
00:17:41,475 --> 00:17:55,807
[SPEAKER_02]: Even when you... like when you fully withdraw yourself from sensuality in the first jhāna, it is a permanent abandonment that doesn't last forever, but like within that time, it is permanent, so to say.

174
00:17:56,687 --> 00:18:04,274
[SPEAKER_02]: It sounds like a paradox, but it's like... what it means is, you factually, at that moment, don't have any inclination, even for the future.

175
00:18:05,302 --> 00:18:05,662
[SPEAKER_02]: To do it.

176
00:18:06,543 --> 00:18:07,384
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's not like that.

177
00:18:07,424 --> 00:18:09,585
[SPEAKER_02]: You have planned beforehand.

178
00:18:09,846 --> 00:18:11,567
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, only for the next two hours.

179
00:18:12,408 --> 00:18:23,156
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why it's... Actually, the sutta says, it's not up to a person to decide, I will enter the first jhāna, nor is it for them, up to them to decide when I will leave the first jhāna.

180
00:18:23,596 --> 00:18:25,157
[SPEAKER_02]: And people would think, oh, that's...

181
00:18:26,503 --> 00:18:33,904
[SPEAKER_02]: That's like the state with your eyes closed and stuff that, you know, you're just locked in there and you can't come out of it.

182
00:18:33,964 --> 00:18:34,084
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

183
00:18:34,104 --> 00:18:44,586
[SPEAKER_02]: What it means is, you won't be able to delight, you won't be able to decide when the sensual desire will come back, like when the hindrances will come back.

184
00:18:45,166 --> 00:18:54,508
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if you're living out there in the world and doing stuff, the state will remain for as long as, depending on the strength of the establishment.

185
00:18:55,248 --> 00:19:01,981
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that, in that sense, it's like indefinitely stretched towards the future.

186
00:19:04,606 --> 00:19:06,650
[SPEAKER_00]: Just to come back a little bit on the...

187
00:19:07,332 --> 00:19:11,855
[SPEAKER_00]: The analogy you made, like with the... someone that goes to the shop to buy, like, a cigarette.

188
00:19:12,555 --> 00:19:24,802
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it's literally, like, impossible for him to see, like, this inclination that he have, even though, like, he's not with the cigarette yet, but he's wanting to have them, if he's already in the car smoking a cigarette.

189
00:19:25,503 --> 00:19:25,963
[SPEAKER_00]: You know what I mean?

190
00:19:25,983 --> 00:19:30,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if he doesn't abandon, like, this kind of gross...

191
00:19:30,946 --> 00:19:34,388
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, he want to abandon cigarettes, so, obviously, he have to put down the cigarette's top,

192
00:19:35,117 --> 00:19:37,578
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, smoking it in order to see his inclination.

193
00:19:37,938 --> 00:19:39,739
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's the same with the virtue and the precept.

194
00:19:39,779 --> 00:19:46,381
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if we don't abandon, like, the gross stuff first, it's literally impossible to see, like, the inclination.

195
00:19:46,521 --> 00:19:49,502
[SPEAKER_02]: And not just, like, particularly, as I said, abandoning the actions.

196
00:19:49,542 --> 00:19:52,403
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, abandon planning to do it.

197
00:19:52,943 --> 00:19:54,384
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that's how then...

198
00:19:54,404 --> 00:19:57,165
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, you abandon planning to do it and then you see...

199
00:19:57,225 --> 00:20:03,307
[SPEAKER_02]: Even then, you have, like, um... That's where the... the... How to put it?

200
00:20:03,347 --> 00:20:03,707
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like...

201
00:20:05,705 --> 00:20:18,768
[SPEAKER_02]: Like an animal that was passing through a gate, unhindered, every time, and now you put the gate there, and now you start hearing the animal knocking against the gate.

202
00:20:19,208 --> 00:20:22,508
[SPEAKER_02]: So you know exactly where was the point where it was crossing.

203
00:20:22,949 --> 00:20:27,509
[SPEAKER_02]: But before, like, you have no idea where it is that it's passing through.

204
00:20:27,529 --> 00:20:28,410
[SPEAKER_02]: So it's like a fence.

205
00:20:28,450 --> 00:20:29,470
[SPEAKER_02]: You have to fence it off,

206
00:20:30,432 --> 00:20:32,994
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, you'll be able to find out the tracks.

207
00:20:33,074 --> 00:20:34,875
[SPEAKER_02]: This is where it used to come in through.

208
00:20:35,435 --> 00:20:40,618
[SPEAKER_02]: But before that, you might be guarding as much as you want.

209
00:20:41,538 --> 00:20:45,861
[SPEAKER_02]: You have three fences.

210
00:20:45,881 --> 00:20:49,523
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, you're standing in the middle there.

211
00:20:50,825 --> 00:20:52,827
[SPEAKER_02]: With guns or whatever, it doesn't matter.

212
00:20:53,287 --> 00:20:57,431
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, there is space for whoever wants to come in, to go in.

213
00:20:57,911 --> 00:21:15,947
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's when people would be... For example, you haven't gone into that part yet, but doing the mindfulness and recollection and awareness, that it would say, like, when you're bending your arm, walking, sitting, speaking, you do so with mindfulness.

214
00:21:16,627 --> 00:21:30,277
[SPEAKER_02]: And that would be like... yeah, so it's guarding the gateway super, you know, judiciously, forgetting that actually the whole property, there's still plenty of room for anybody without you noticing.

215
00:21:30,357 --> 00:21:34,421
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, step one is like... Fencing it off, yeah.

216
00:21:34,481 --> 00:21:38,664
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but people often take it to be like, all right, step one, I have to be like, yeah, you know.

217
00:21:39,124 --> 00:21:44,066
[SPEAKER_01]: Every time I move a little bit, you know, I gotta be paying attention.

218
00:21:44,166 --> 00:21:58,813
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why, see, you would be misconceiving what that even means, even though you're reading it and it sort of makes sense, but you're misconceiving what it actually means just because you haven't developed those prerequisites beforehand.

219
00:22:08,153 --> 00:22:14,316
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when the bhikkhu has his sense doors guarded, the realized one guides him further.

220
00:22:14,536 --> 00:22:16,217
[SPEAKER_01]: Come, bhikkhu, be moderate in eating.

221
00:22:16,818 --> 00:22:27,003
[SPEAKER_01]: Eat reflecting through the origin, not for fun, indulgence, adornment, or decoration, but only to sustain this body, to avoid harm, and to support the renunciate life.

222
00:22:27,643 --> 00:22:34,327
[SPEAKER_01]: In this way, I shall put an end to old discomfort and not give rise to new discomfort, and I will live blamelessly and at ease.

223
00:22:36,636 --> 00:22:43,467
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's a more specific way in which you will be grasping the signs and features.

224
00:22:46,223 --> 00:22:51,726
[SPEAKER_02]: With food, you have no say in the fact that food will be pleasant.

225
00:22:52,427 --> 00:23:02,412
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you can decide to only eat... like the like the Jains were doing, like, you know, sometimes, I think, even eating dirt and leaves and stuff.

226
00:23:03,333 --> 00:23:09,576
[SPEAKER_02]: And sure, in the beginning, it's going to be disgusting, but eventually, at some point, you get used to it, and it's like the stuff that...

227
00:23:11,070 --> 00:23:15,552
[SPEAKER_02]: Just the plain fact of eating is going to carry pleasure with it.

228
00:23:15,752 --> 00:23:19,774
[SPEAKER_02]: So then, that's actually quite a integral

229
00:23:25,480 --> 00:23:34,208
[SPEAKER_02]: It would very acutely show you the principle of how to not have passion even though pleasant feeling is there.

230
00:23:34,368 --> 00:23:47,180
[SPEAKER_02]: So, instead of like, you know, starting again with meditation where you wouldn't really see how is it that you can have feelings arising on their own without you delighting in them.

231
00:23:48,701 --> 00:23:52,582
[SPEAKER_02]: This would actually teach you that, before you get to that point.

232
00:23:52,642 --> 00:24:06,047
[SPEAKER_02]: You would realize, yeah, I have no say in the fact that sometimes food will be pleasant, but then you recognize, I actually do have a choice in whether I'm eating that food, as it says there, for fun, for beautification, for pleasure, and...

233
00:24:08,582 --> 00:24:14,945
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, most often, even, you would be eating past the point where you're actually satisfying the hunger.

234
00:24:15,705 --> 00:24:24,810
[SPEAKER_02]: You would be eating, like, as it says, that creating new discomfort, like overeating, just because your motivation for eating was wrong.

235
00:24:24,850 --> 00:24:34,834
[SPEAKER_02]: It was to get as much of this pleasure as you want, and obviously, then, you will overshoot beyond the point of... practical point of keeping yourself alive.

236
00:24:35,615 --> 00:24:35,895
[SPEAKER_02]: So,

237
00:24:37,750 --> 00:24:42,474
[SPEAKER_02]: But, again, like, you wouldn't... That's another one, actually.

238
00:24:43,234 --> 00:24:54,343
[SPEAKER_02]: When in retreats and stuff, you would see people, like, eating super slowly and, you know, trying to be extra mindful of every single morsel and feeling it.

239
00:24:55,524 --> 00:24:57,385
[SPEAKER_01]: See, that's already misconceiving.

240
00:24:58,366 --> 00:24:59,567
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't talk about that.

241
00:24:59,607 --> 00:25:05,611
[SPEAKER_02]: It says, be clear about your motivation, that it's not for fun, not for beautification, not for pleasure.

242
00:25:06,640 --> 00:25:22,711
[SPEAKER_02]: And that would only be be apparent, like those subtle motivations, through sensory strength, because it's through grasping the wrong signs and features that are there, in relation to the food, that you would be doing it out of those wrong reasons.

243
00:25:23,531 --> 00:25:25,432
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's the same thing.

244
00:25:25,552 --> 00:25:29,355
[SPEAKER_02]: The signs and features of agreeability will be there.

245
00:25:30,428 --> 00:25:35,432
[SPEAKER_02]: But because you learned the sense restraint beforehand, now you'll be able to apply it even on that level.

246
00:25:36,373 --> 00:25:43,239
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's interesting, because the sense restraint, you know, it does talk about, kind of, tongue faculty.

247
00:25:44,700 --> 00:25:45,561
[SPEAKER_01]: So that, like...

248
00:25:49,299 --> 00:25:57,286
[SPEAKER_01]: And also, if you're following the Eight Precepts, the eating is kind of implicit in every other step that came before this one.

249
00:25:58,086 --> 00:26:01,950
[SPEAKER_02]: So, there's kind of, like, a very... Yeah.

250
00:26:02,350 --> 00:26:05,012
[SPEAKER_02]: Because there can still be a refinement to it.

251
00:26:05,092 --> 00:26:11,277
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, yeah, if you're keeping Eight Precepts, for example, you're not eating, you would maybe not necessarily eat

252
00:26:14,071 --> 00:26:23,324
[SPEAKER_02]: But you would definitely be more... like you wouldn't be snacking, you know, randomly, like a lot of people do.

253
00:26:23,344 --> 00:26:23,845
[SPEAKER_02]: Like just...

254
00:26:24,045 --> 00:26:26,509
[SPEAKER_02]: I feel bored and I take something from the fridge.

255
00:26:26,649 --> 00:26:27,751
[SPEAKER_02]: That wouldn't be...

256
00:26:28,792 --> 00:26:33,194
[SPEAKER_02]: If you're practicing sense-restraint or seeing the danger in the sight as fault, you wouldn't be doing that.

257
00:26:33,234 --> 00:26:43,178
[SPEAKER_02]: But even then, for a monk, like, you know, eating one meal a day and stuff, you could still overlook that right motivation for eating.

258
00:26:43,218 --> 00:26:45,799
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, again, the defilements are beginningless.

259
00:26:45,879 --> 00:26:52,922
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not that you need to consciously be nurturing passion and light in your mind for it to set in.

260
00:26:53,022 --> 00:26:54,723
[SPEAKER_02]: All you need to do is have pleasant feeling,

261
00:26:55,572 --> 00:26:58,294
[SPEAKER_02]: And not have the right context in regard to it, and lust is there.

262
00:26:58,695 --> 00:27:00,376
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, it is immediately there.

263
00:27:01,137 --> 00:27:03,599
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the same applies with this.

264
00:27:03,639 --> 00:27:11,986
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, if you don't... you might even be practicing sense-restraint in terms of not grasping the signs and features of it, but...

265
00:27:13,057 --> 00:27:15,939
[SPEAKER_02]: Realistically speaking, you still end up eating more than you need.

266
00:27:17,800 --> 00:27:27,807
[SPEAKER_02]: You still end up emphasizing what food you prefer and other stuff, losing perspective of the actual purpose.

267
00:27:39,774 --> 00:27:40,095
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

268
00:27:40,999 --> 00:27:44,221
[SPEAKER_01]: When the bhikkhu eats in moderation, the realized one guides him further.

269
00:27:44,581 --> 00:27:46,442
[SPEAKER_01]: Come bhikkhu, be committed to vigilance.

270
00:27:47,043 --> 00:27:50,585
[SPEAKER_01]: During the day, cleanse your mind of obstructive states while walking or sitting.

271
00:27:51,085 --> 00:27:55,208
[SPEAKER_01]: During the first watch of the night, cleanse your mind of obstructive states while walking or sitting.

272
00:27:55,708 --> 00:28:06,255
[SPEAKER_01]: During the second watch of the night, you should lie down in the lion's posture on the right side, placing one foot on top of the other, recollected and aware, having set the intention to get up.

273
00:28:06,835 --> 00:28:12,792
[SPEAKER_01]: Having gotten up in the last watch of the night, continue to cleanse your mind of obstructive states while walking or sitting.

274
00:28:14,637 --> 00:28:15,058
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so...

275
00:28:16,478 --> 00:28:34,212
[SPEAKER_02]: The obvious reason, of course, why that would be after the moderation eating is because some suttas would even talk about how there would be some monks who would eat... they would just pig out during the meal, and then the immediate impulse would be to go to sleep for the... right after the meal.

276
00:28:34,252 --> 00:28:36,614
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, obviously, you wouldn't be able to do this.

277
00:28:36,734 --> 00:28:38,355
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you would just be...

278
00:28:40,157 --> 00:28:44,824
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why it comes after the moderation in eating.

279
00:28:46,166 --> 00:28:48,250
[SPEAKER_02]: But then, that's already where the...

280
00:28:49,215 --> 00:28:57,599
[SPEAKER_02]: So, see, your sense restraints are definitely not planning to break any of your precepts.

281
00:28:58,860 --> 00:29:06,144
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's already quite a pure state compared to what you had before.

282
00:29:06,344 --> 00:29:16,529
[SPEAKER_02]: It might not feel like it, because, you know, when you're there, there's this pleasure that you have now, always feels like this is it.

283
00:29:17,947 --> 00:29:22,109
[SPEAKER_02]: You never have, like, perspective to compare how it was in the past.

284
00:29:22,249 --> 00:29:23,469
[SPEAKER_02]: All you have is what you have now.

285
00:29:23,529 --> 00:29:25,890
[SPEAKER_02]: So, this will feel like it's a lot.

286
00:29:26,170 --> 00:29:31,892
[SPEAKER_02]: But actually, it's much, like, just... not necessarily do it, but...

287
00:29:33,285 --> 00:29:48,154
[SPEAKER_02]: You can know, if I were to go back to breaking the precepts, there would be a significant difference in the amount of wildness and tameness that my mind exhibits.

288
00:29:48,214 --> 00:29:55,138
[SPEAKER_02]: So, basically, what I'm saying is that already involves a degree of samādhi, being in that state.

289
00:29:55,338 --> 00:29:56,919
[SPEAKER_02]: But, you can go further.

290
00:29:56,979 --> 00:29:58,340
[SPEAKER_02]: So, obstructive states.

291
00:30:01,149 --> 00:30:02,193
[SPEAKER_02]: Intentions.

292
00:30:02,374 --> 00:30:07,271
[SPEAKER_02]: That's where you would be starting to look at these more subtle things.

293
00:30:08,089 --> 00:30:24,760
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, you can be having still worry, still desires, still preoccupations, doubts about the dhamma, about wholesome things, quote-unquote, and that would still be an obstructive state.

294
00:30:25,561 --> 00:30:27,362
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can start looking at that.

295
00:30:27,382 --> 00:30:33,246
[SPEAKER_02]: So, within this restrained setup, what do I spend my time thinking about?

296
00:30:34,758 --> 00:30:39,462
[SPEAKER_02]: Whether it's during the day, during the evening, and after I wake up.

297
00:30:39,622 --> 00:30:41,044
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, that's the other thing.

298
00:30:41,064 --> 00:30:42,905
[SPEAKER_02]: Sleep.

299
00:30:43,826 --> 00:30:47,950
[SPEAKER_02]: Quite often, like, you go to sleep and it's sort of like you give up.

300
00:30:48,330 --> 00:30:56,758
[SPEAKER_02]: You kind of let go and then... Again, you don't necessarily need to want to enjoy the sleep.

301
00:30:57,623 --> 00:31:04,608
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just automatic that, as other suttas would say, you would be indulging in the pleasure of lying down.

302
00:31:04,648 --> 00:31:13,033
[SPEAKER_02]: But it would say, yeah, you go to sleep, but then, as soon as you are awake, you get up.

303
00:31:13,533 --> 00:31:19,637
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, otherwise, yeah, automatically, that would be you indulging in that pleasure of sleepiness.

304
00:31:19,677 --> 00:31:25,461
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you already woke up, but you choose to just...

305
00:31:25,721 --> 00:31:26,442
[SPEAKER_02]: I'll just keep going.

306
00:31:28,669 --> 00:31:42,835
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a... that kind of simple instruction of noting the time for waking, right when you go to bed, it kind of nips this subtle tendency right in the bud.

307
00:31:43,475 --> 00:31:45,096
[SPEAKER_01]: This tendency of just

308
00:31:46,484 --> 00:31:51,045
[SPEAKER_01]: You kind of just go to sleep and just are hoping that it'll last forever.

309
00:31:51,346 --> 00:31:51,606
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

310
00:31:51,646 --> 00:31:53,726
[SPEAKER_01]: In a way, it's just like, oh, this is gonna be great.

311
00:31:53,746 --> 00:31:58,028
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, you like, you just, there's no kind of, like, foresight.

312
00:31:58,348 --> 00:31:58,488
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

313
00:31:58,508 --> 00:31:59,928
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just like, let's just... Yeah.

314
00:32:00,088 --> 00:32:00,268
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

315
00:32:01,069 --> 00:32:01,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

316
00:32:01,529 --> 00:32:03,389
[SPEAKER_02]: It's... but even then, it's...

317
00:32:03,989 --> 00:32:09,351
[SPEAKER_02]: I wouldn't necessarily translate it as noting the time for waking up, because that kind of implies that you have a say.

318
00:32:09,971 --> 00:32:13,212
[SPEAKER_02]: Sometimes you might sleep for eight hours or more, because you're factually tired.

319
00:32:13,733 --> 00:32:14,533
[SPEAKER_02]: It's more like...

320
00:32:16,134 --> 00:32:19,216
[SPEAKER_02]: Having the intention to wake up, as it says.

321
00:32:19,797 --> 00:32:28,404
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's the determination that you will wake up, but you will get up once you wake up.

322
00:32:29,265 --> 00:32:31,687
[SPEAKER_02]: Because sometimes people could take that in the wrong ways.

323
00:32:32,568 --> 00:32:40,915
[SPEAKER_02]: Predetermining the amount of sleep that they will have, as often happens, I think, in meditation retreats again.

324
00:32:40,995 --> 00:32:45,839
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you're expected to exactly wake up at the same time and go to sleep at the same time, but...

325
00:32:45,939 --> 00:32:50,363
[SPEAKER_01]: Right at the mathematical, like, okay, second watch of the night, time to fall asleep.

326
00:32:50,683 --> 00:32:50,843
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

327
00:32:50,883 --> 00:32:53,125
[SPEAKER_02]: Third watch of the night, all right, I'm up right away.

328
00:32:53,305 --> 00:32:57,769
[SPEAKER_02]: But ideally, yeah, you want to recognize your body has its own needs and your...

329
00:33:02,172 --> 00:33:05,614
[SPEAKER_02]: The light begins when you go beyond what the body needs.

330
00:33:06,395 --> 00:33:08,076
[SPEAKER_02]: Same with the food.

331
00:33:08,296 --> 00:33:18,823
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not necessarily lustful to eat, but when you eat beyond the basic need of survival and being able to practice, that's when it turns into something else.

332
00:33:18,943 --> 00:33:27,049
[SPEAKER_01]: Or would it be fair to say that that is when it kind of is always going to be delight?

333
00:33:27,822 --> 00:33:34,486
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you could just eat what you need to survive, but still be delighting in it, and sleeping just as much as you need, right?

334
00:33:34,506 --> 00:33:35,386
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

335
00:33:35,426 --> 00:33:40,209
[SPEAKER_02]: That's when it's definitely going to be ruining and wholesome, so...

336
00:33:46,372 --> 00:33:50,495
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when the bhikkhu is committed to vigilance, the realized one guides him further.

337
00:33:51,180 --> 00:33:51,740
[SPEAKER_01]: kāmbeku.

338
00:33:51,901 --> 00:33:54,022
[SPEAKER_01]: Be possessed of recollection and awareness.

339
00:33:54,623 --> 00:34:13,978
[SPEAKER_01]: Act with awareness when going out and coming back, when looking ahead and aside, when bending and extending the limbs, when bearing the outer robe, bowl, and robes, when eating, drinking, chewing, and tasting, when urinating and defecating, when walking, standing, sitting, sleeping, walking, speaking, and keeping silent.

340
00:34:14,838 --> 00:34:16,299
[SPEAKER_01]: Waking and keeping silent.

341
00:34:19,365 --> 00:34:22,827
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's where that comes in now.

342
00:34:23,808 --> 00:34:32,913
[SPEAKER_02]: Even within that vigilance that you have on a more broader scope, like, what am I spending my time thinking about?

343
00:34:32,953 --> 00:34:34,514
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you can refine it even further.

344
00:34:34,574 --> 00:34:41,798
[SPEAKER_02]: Like every little movement you make, now you have a clarity about the intention behind it.

345
00:34:41,898 --> 00:34:44,840
[SPEAKER_02]: Not like watching the sensation of my elbow.

