01:01:15,817 --> 01:01:18,476
[SPEAKER_02]: Ultimately, even craving for the dhamma, you would abandon.

578
01:01:19,884 --> 01:01:21,705
[SPEAKER_02]: But not before fulfilling these things.

579
01:01:21,905 --> 01:01:26,927
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, it would come as a result of... all this understanding would come as a result of these things.

580
01:01:26,947 --> 01:01:29,768
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why they would say the dhamma is like a raft.

581
01:01:30,348 --> 01:01:36,971
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you can't drop the raft before you have actually gone any distance with it.

582
01:01:37,231 --> 01:01:37,911
[SPEAKER_02]: So...

583
01:01:38,432 --> 01:01:41,573
[SPEAKER_02]: If you want to clean a house, this is something I've thought about.

584
01:01:41,593 --> 01:01:46,315
[SPEAKER_01]: Because people are like, you know, isn't wanting to free yourself from suffering, craving.

585
01:01:47,095 --> 01:01:55,118
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, if you want to clean a house, like, remove everything and make it spotless, first step is certainly not to remove the cleaning supplies.

586
01:01:55,418 --> 01:02:03,020
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

587
01:02:03,240 --> 01:02:07,162
[SPEAKER_02]: And I think there's another... there's a simile he gave there.

588
01:02:07,522 --> 01:02:10,983
[SPEAKER_02]: It would be quite useful to read.

589
01:02:20,421 --> 01:02:27,303
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the brahman essentially asks, why do some people get it and some people don't?

590
01:02:28,583 --> 01:02:29,383
[SPEAKER_01]: And...

591
01:02:29,443 --> 01:02:32,684
[SPEAKER_01]: Some people, even though they instruct, the Buddha instructs...

592
01:02:32,704 --> 01:02:36,025
[SPEAKER_01]: Even though they're given this instruction from the Buddha himself.

593
01:02:36,945 --> 01:02:38,825
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he says, what do you think, brahman?

594
01:02:39,306 --> 01:02:43,247
[SPEAKER_01]: Suppose a person was to come along, who wanted to go to Rājagaha.

595
01:02:44,007 --> 01:02:46,427
[SPEAKER_01]: He'd approach you and say, sir, I wish to go to Rājagaha.

596
01:02:46,807 --> 01:02:48,628
[SPEAKER_01]: Please point out the road to Rājagaha.

597
01:02:49,368 --> 01:02:50,569
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I skipped the point.

598
01:02:50,809 --> 01:02:54,850
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha asks if he's familiar with the way to Rājagaha, which he is.

599
01:02:55,871 --> 01:03:00,092
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you would say to them, here, mister, this road goes to Rājagaha.

600
01:03:00,613 --> 01:03:02,974
[SPEAKER_01]: Go along it for a while, you'll see a certain village.

601
01:03:03,374 --> 01:03:05,835
[SPEAKER_01]: Go along a while further, you'll see a certain town.

602
01:03:06,475 --> 01:03:12,758
[SPEAKER_01]: Go along a while further, and you'll see Rājagaha with its delightful parks, woods, meadows, and lotus ponds.

603
01:03:13,438 --> 01:03:17,900
[SPEAKER_01]: Instructed like this by you, they might still take the wrong road heading west.

604
01:03:19,011 --> 01:03:23,753
[SPEAKER_01]: But a second person might come with the same question, receive the same instructions.

605
01:03:23,833 --> 01:03:26,915
[SPEAKER_01]: Instructed by you, they might safely arrive at Rājagāha.

606
01:03:27,535 --> 01:03:28,576
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the cause, Brahman?

607
01:03:29,056 --> 01:03:36,779
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the reason why, though Rājagāha is present, the path leading to Rājagāha is present, and you are there to encourage them?

608
01:03:37,300 --> 01:03:41,982
[SPEAKER_01]: One person takes the wrong path and heads west, while another arrives safely at Rājagāha.

609
01:03:42,829 --> 01:03:44,589
[SPEAKER_01]: What can I do about that, Master Gautama?

610
01:03:45,550 --> 01:03:47,190
[SPEAKER_01]: I am the one who shows the way.

611
01:03:48,030 --> 01:04:02,534
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same way, though nibbāna is available, the path leading to nibbāna is available, and I am present to encourage them, still some of my disciples, instructed and advised like this, achieve the ultimate goal, nibbāna, while some of them fail.

612
01:04:03,134 --> 01:04:04,494
[SPEAKER_01]: What can I do about that, Brahman?

613
01:04:04,854 --> 01:04:06,895
[SPEAKER_01]: The realized one is the one who shows the way.

614
01:04:09,309 --> 01:04:09,509
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

615
01:04:09,769 --> 01:04:24,733
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's... if you're given directions to go somewhere, and you were supposed to turn left, like, after having turned left, right, left, right, ten times, but you start with that, what's that going to result in?

616
01:04:24,993 --> 01:04:27,134
[SPEAKER_02]: You won't end up in the place you were supposed to be in.

617
01:04:27,874 --> 01:04:29,314
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the same applies with this.

618
01:04:29,354 --> 01:04:38,197
[SPEAKER_02]: If you try to do any of these steps before the others, you try to shift the order or something, you won't end up in

619
01:04:40,455 --> 01:04:40,715
[SPEAKER_02]: Right.

620
01:04:41,536 --> 01:04:45,638
[SPEAKER_02]: So, there's a... just in kind of my rock-climbing background...

621
01:04:59,345 --> 01:05:24,705
[SPEAKER_01]: Near where I lived for a while, there's a big cliff and there's a route on it called Sam Swan Song, and it's a relatively easy route in terms of, like, it's not physically difficult, but the, um, like, if you're an experienced climber, you're used to kind of looking for certain, like, cracks and features in the rock, and essentially what makes this so difficult is that

622
01:05:26,167 --> 01:05:32,038
[SPEAKER_01]: If you go based on that instant instinct, you will be deceived into some pretty horrendous situations.

623
01:05:32,679 --> 01:05:36,526
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need, like, very specific instructions for how to get up this thing.

624
01:05:37,128 --> 01:05:38,109
[SPEAKER_01]: And every single

625
01:05:39,417 --> 01:05:44,702
[SPEAKER_01]: Bit of it is essentially having you go against your gut instinct as a climber.

626
01:05:45,563 --> 01:05:49,086
[SPEAKER_02]: So, there's like this similar analogy.

627
01:05:49,727 --> 01:05:57,935
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, because the whole instinct is, like, if you didn't have the instruction from the Buddha, there's, like, very little chance.

628
01:05:58,035 --> 01:06:01,778
[SPEAKER_02]: And you might cultivate, like, as it would say in the past,

629
01:06:02,783 --> 01:06:06,247
[SPEAKER_02]: Ascetics and brahmins would keep the precepts.

630
01:06:06,347 --> 01:06:11,092
[SPEAKER_02]: But, yeah, it would be enough for them to end up in heaven and stuff.

631
01:06:11,152 --> 01:06:18,419
[SPEAKER_02]: But, even to get to the point of samādhi, like, it would be very few that would find out how to do that on their own.

632
01:06:19,280 --> 01:06:24,205
[SPEAKER_02]: So, yeah, your instinct will almost always lead you to the wrong direction.

633
01:06:24,525 --> 01:06:25,887
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, the thing is, like,

634
01:06:29,128 --> 01:06:39,814
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not that, as I mentioned before, it's not that there is some magical force that's stopping you from getting to the goal.

635
01:06:40,054 --> 01:06:48,659
[SPEAKER_02]: It's really the fact that if you already knew even what you're supposed to do, you would already be there.

636
01:06:49,899 --> 01:06:52,561
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's not like I already know what I'm supposed to do

637
01:06:53,787 --> 01:06:59,592
[SPEAKER_02]: But still, somehow... Like, the fourth noble truth is the knowledge of the path to nibbāna.

638
01:07:00,392 --> 01:07:03,595
[SPEAKER_02]: And it says that whoever sees the four noble truths, sees all the others.

639
01:07:04,235 --> 01:07:06,998
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you know how to get there, you already got there.

640
01:07:08,699 --> 01:07:09,339
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the whole thing.

641
01:07:09,439 --> 01:07:13,843
[SPEAKER_02]: So... That's why the instruction of another is essential.

642
01:07:13,923 --> 01:07:17,766
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not that you can... Like, I do know how to get there, but I haven't really.

643
01:07:18,787 --> 01:07:19,287
[SPEAKER_02]: That's not...

644
01:07:21,878 --> 01:07:35,721
[SPEAKER_02]: Even for a noble disciple, he might have a general idea of what the arahantship is, but the fact that he doesn't have it yet means he's not clear enough about how he should go about developing it.

645
01:07:36,122 --> 01:07:40,983
[SPEAKER_02]: He knows the outline, the blueprint to build a house, but...

646
01:07:42,120 --> 01:07:50,588
[SPEAKER_02]: He's not necessarily sure about why, you know, I put this here, but it's still kind of wobbly, and it doesn't really seem like it fits.

647
01:07:51,269 --> 01:07:54,992
[SPEAKER_02]: Eventually, he'll figure it out, inevitably, at least within seven lifetimes.

648
01:07:55,593 --> 01:07:59,356
[SPEAKER_02]: But fundamentally, if you know exactly how to build the house,

649
01:08:00,732 --> 01:08:03,696
[SPEAKER_02]: Every single step of the way, it's because you already built the house.

650
01:08:05,018 --> 01:08:13,410
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why you can never put aside the the need for for a stone of instruction.

651
01:08:13,610 --> 01:08:17,235
[SPEAKER_02]: And you can never think like, like, oh, I already figured it out or something.

652
01:08:19,298 --> 01:08:31,103
[SPEAKER_00]: I found interesting that, also, that the Buddha, when he shared this simile, he doesn't just say, oh, yeah, like, the road is there, carry along and you will get there.

653
01:08:31,223 --> 01:08:38,406
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, like, actually, like, again, as a gradual thing, like, first you will meet a village, don't stop there, there will be a town, don't stop there, and then you will get there.

654
01:08:38,566 --> 01:08:40,247
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's kind of, like, interesting as well.

655
01:08:40,287 --> 01:08:46,330
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, because sometimes you would be told, oh, just, just, as long as you're following what is wholesome, you will just get there.

656
01:08:46,770 --> 01:08:49,352
[SPEAKER_02]: As if you already know what is wholesome from the start.

657
01:08:50,293 --> 01:08:56,618
[SPEAKER_01]: That was in the very last thing, is putting aside doubt about what is wholesome.

658
01:08:56,738 --> 01:09:01,622
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, there's no kind of questioning about what is and isn't wholesome.

659
01:09:01,823 --> 01:09:08,448
[SPEAKER_02]: Not by finding answers, but by realizing, oh, what's wholesome is not to resist the discomfort of not knowing the answer.

660
01:09:09,068 --> 01:09:10,550
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the answer to everything, basically.

